This is an approximation of converting assertions to LINQPad or .NET 'Debug.Assert' statements from a similar code base. It's only applicable for the given conditions mentioned in the original code.--------------------Certainly! Here are the assertion statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@" in the C# code:

1. For "@@ last index of x is geq an index of x in new array list" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX);
```

This assertion checks if the last index of x in the new array list (New_arrListLastIndexOfX) is greater than or equals to the index of x in the new array list (New_arrListIndexOfX). This could indicate a potential issue if the item is not correctly inserted or removed, leading to a situation where the last index of x is less than the index of x.

2. For "@@ remove should be idempotent" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == !New_arrListContainsX);
```

In this case, we assert that the counts and indices should not change after the item is removed, the presence of the item in the collection should be inverted, due to the idempotency of removal.

3. For "@@ any indexed value remains unchanged" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(index < Old_arrListCount);
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == Old_arrList[index]);
```

This checks whether the index is valid (less than the count of the previous list), and the returned value remains the same as the old list at the index.

4. For "@@ count increase after add or remove" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);
```

This assertion simply checks whether the count in the new array list is one greater than the previous count after an insertion.

5. For "@@ count decrement after remove item" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == (Old_arrListCount - 1));
```

The assertion tests whether the count in the new array list (New_arrListCount) is one less than the previous count (Old_arrListCount) after a successful removal.

6. For "@@ remove first item" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == 0);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == 0);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == false);
```

These assertions help to confirm that the first item in the list has been correctly removed, ensuring that the index, last index and containance state are updated correctly.

7. For "@@ remove last item" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount - 1);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == false);
```

This set of assertions verifies that the last item is removed properly, ensuring that the count, index, last index and containance state are updated correctly.

8. For "@@ insert at the end of the list" comment:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListCount - 1);
Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListCount - 1);
```

These assertions confirm the addition of an item at the end of the list, setting up the index and last index correctly.

